---
import ToolPage from '../../components/ToolPage.astro';
import ContentTable from '../../components/ContentTable.astro';
import { YEAR } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { rel, srcs } from '../../lib/links';
import { pension, taux, MV, MODES } from '../../lib/engine/pension';
import { formatMoney, formatPercent } from '../../lib/format';
const lang = 'en';
const $ = (x: number) => formatMoney(x, 0, lang);
const pc = (x: number) => formatPercent(x, 1, lang);
const N = 4;
const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the table rate for six children?', a: `For six children the table uses ${pc(taux(6, "reduit"))} per child with reduced visiting, ${pc(taux(6, "classique"))} with standard visiting and ${pc(taux(6, "alterne"))} with shared residence, applied to the paying parent’s income minus the subsistence amount. In total, with standard visiting, support is 43.2% of that disposable income, leaving the paying parent a little over half of what exceeds the subsistence amount.` },
  { q: 'Can support for four children push the parent below the RSA?', a: `In principle no: the table first deducts a subsistence amount equal to the single-person RSA, and support only applies to the rest. But that amount ignores rent and other costs, so a parent can be left with very little once housing is paid. That is an argument to put to the judge, with evidence, to obtain less than the table.` },
  { q: 'With seven or more children, what does a French judge do?', a: `The table stops at six children, so there is no official rate beyond. The judge sets the contribution from the children’s needs and each parent’s resources, without a grid. Our calculator applies the six-child rate to each child, which gives an order of magnitude, not a rule.` }
];
---
<ToolPage lang={lang} id="enfant4" kind="enfants-4" breadcrumbLabel="4 children or more"
  title={`Child support for four or more children France ${YEAR}`}
  h1="Child support for four, five or six children in France"
  description={`Child support for 4, 5 or 6 children in France in ${YEAR}: per-child rates from the Ministry of Justice table, the total by income, and where the table stops.`}
  intro="Beyond three children the table carries on up to six, with lower and lower per-child rates."
  resume={`The French Ministry of Justice table covers families of up to six children. With standard visiting, the per-child rate is ${pc(taux(4, "classique"))} for four children, ${pc(taux(5, "classique"))} for five and ${pc(taux(6, "classique"))} for six. The total keeps rising: 35.2%, 40% then 43.2% of income above the subsistence amount. On ${$(3000)} net with four children, indicative support is ${$(pension(3000, 4, "classique").total)} a month. At these levels the subsistence amount of ${$(MV)} is often the paying parent’s only real protection, and the judge looks closely at what they have left. Beyond six children the table stops: our tool then keeps the six-child rate, a simple calculation convention. For large families the judge’s own assessment counts more than ever. The calculator below works for four to six children.`}
  faqs={faqs} related={rel(['enfant3', 'juge', 'home', 'bareme', 'amount-3000', 'chomage'], lang)} sources={srcs(['bareme', 'pension', 'rsa'], lang)}>
  <h2>The limits of the table for large families</h2>
  <p>The bigger the family, the further the table drifts from real budgets: it sees neither the higher family benefits received by the parent raising the children nor the cost of a four-bedroom home. A decision for a large family can therefore depart from the scale, and negotiation between parents is better grounded in each home’s actual budget. The calculator compares the three visiting columns; <a href={route("juge", lang)}>how judges decide</a> lists what justifies a departure.</p>
  <h2>Support for four to six children by income</h2>
  <ContentTable caption="Total monthly support" headers={['Net income', '4 children', '5 children', '6 children']} align={['l', 'r', 'r', 'r']} rows={[1500, 2000, 2500, 3000, 3500, 4000, 5000, 6000].map((x) => [$(x), ...[4, 5, 6].map((k) => $(pension(x, k, 'classique').total))])} />
  <p>Amounts are those of the reference table, rounded to the euro. For another income, use the calculator above or the <a href={route('home', lang)}>full calculator</a>, which adds the ASF and the tax effect.</p>
</ToolPage>
